      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "To prevent ice dams on your Utah roof, seal warm air leaks in your attic and upgrade insulation to the recommended Utah level of R-49.",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "seal warm air leaks in your attic and upgrade insulation to the recommended Utah level of R-49"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Ice dams happen when escaping indoor heat warms the roof deck, melting snow that then refreezes upon reaching the colder eaves. Managing Utah's heavy winter snow requires a combination of home efficiency, proper maintenance, and targeted tools."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Key Prevention Strategies"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Upgrade Attic Insulation: Bring your attic insulation up to R-49, which is the standard recommendation for Utah homes to stop heat from escaping through the roof deck ."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Seal Air Leaks: Caulk and seal gaps around attic penetrations, plumbing vents, light fixtures, chimneys, and the attic hatch."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Maintain Roof Ventilation: Ensure your soffit vents and ridge or gable vents are clear of insulation and debris so cool outside air can circulate and keep the entire roof deck uniformly cold."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Clean Gutters: Clear out leaves and twigs before the first freeze so melting snow has a clear path to drain away from your home."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Use a Roof Rake: Pull fresh snow off the lower 3 to 4 feet of your roofline using a long-handled roof rake after heavy Utah storms to eliminate the snow feeding the dam."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Install Heat Tape: Wrap zig-zag patterns of electric heat cable (heat tape) along problem eaves and valleys to maintain a drainage channel."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Professional Services in Utah"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If you need local assistance with winter roof preparation, inspections, or active removal:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Inspections & Maintenance: Companies like Reimagine Roofing provide seasonal winterization, flashing checks, and roof tune-ups.",
            "snippet_links": [
              {
                "text": "Reimagine Roofing",
                "link": "https://www.reimagineroofing.com/blog/winter-roof-prep-in-utah/"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Protective Treatments: Contractors such as Superior Roofing Systems offer specialized shingle coatings designed to help defend against severe winter weathering and ice damage."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Heat Cable Installation: Regional providers like Lion Flow specialize in setting up custom heat tape systems along rooflines across the Wasatch Front and Back."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Safe Removal: If an ice dam already forms, avoid using sharp tools or high-pressure washers that ruin shingles; instead, look for experienced local professionals who use low-pressure, high-temperature steam (around 100 PSI and 285\u00b0F)."
          }
        ]
      },
